---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const structuredData = {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
    name: 'PixlPut',
    applicationCategory: 'UtilityApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'macOS 14+',
    offers: {
        '@type': 'Offer',
        price: '19',
        priceCurrency: 'USD',
        availability: 'https://schema.org/InStock',
    },
    description: 'PixlPut remembers where every window was, on every Desktop, and puts each one back after sleep, wake, display changes and restarts.',
    softwareVersion: '1.0.3',
};
---
<BaseLayout
    title="PixlPut — Window memory for macOS"
    description="PixlPut remembers where every window was, on every Desktop, and puts each one back after sleep, wake, display changes and restarts. Your layouts never leave your Mac."
    path="/"
    structuredData={structuredData}
>
    <section class="hero container">
        <h1>Your windows. Where you left them.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            Sleep, wake, lock, restart: macOS scatters your carefully arranged windows across the wrong Desktops and the wrong corners.
            PixlPut remembers where each one belongs and puts it back. Arrange once, capture once, stop rearranging.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
            <a href="/buy" class="btn btn-primary">Get PixlPut · $19 one-time</a>
            <a href="#features" class="btn btn-secondary">See features</a>
        </div>
        <p class="micro">14-day free trial · No account · Developer ID signed and notarized · macOS 14 or later</p>
    </section>

    <section id="features" class="features container">
        <h2>What PixlPut does</h2>
        <div class="grid">
            <article>
                <h3>Survives sleep &amp; wake</h3>
                <p>Waking from sleep, waking the displays, unlocking, a display change: PixlPut puts your windows back on their own. Windows already in place aren't touched.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>Every Desktop, remembered</h3>
                <p>Each Mission Control Desktop keeps its own layout. Add, remove or reorder Desktops and each layout stays with the Desktop it belongs to.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>Back after a restart</h3>
                <p>After a reboot, PixlPut waits for your apps to finish relaunching, then returns every window to its Desktop and position, including apps that open late.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>Knows which window is which</h3>
                <p>Three browser windows, four VS Code projects? Optional per-app identity matches each window by its tab, workspace or project, so each one returns to its own spot.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>You decide what's saved</h3>
                <p>Your layout changes only when you click Capture. Move a window afterwards and it stays put until the next wake. Snapshot history lets you go back to an earlier layout.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>Local-only by design</h3>
                <p>Layouts live in your Library. No telemetry, no analytics, no cloud sync. The only network calls are license checks and update checks.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="how" class="how container">
        <h2>How it works</h2>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Arrange.</strong> Lay out your windows the way you like them, on as many Desktops as you use.</li>
            <li><strong>Capture.</strong> Click Capture now in the menu bar. PixlPut saves each window's position, size and Desktop.</li>
            <li><strong>Forget about it.</strong> After sleep, wake, a display change or a restart, PixlPut puts everything back. The Desktop on screen is restored right away, and every other Desktop the first time you visit it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="aside">Built on Apple's Accessibility API. Signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple, so Gatekeeper opens it without warnings.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="pricing" class="pricing container">
        <h2>One price. No subscription.</h2>
        <div class="plan">
            <div class="price">$19</div>
            <div class="meta">One-time · 3 Macs · Free updates for 1 year</div>
            <ul>
                <li>Restore on sleep, wake, unlock, display change and restart</li>
                <li>Separate layouts for every Desktop, plus snapshot history</li>
                <li>14-day full-featured trial</li>
                <li>Developer ID signed &amp; notarized</li>
                <li>Email support, refund within 30 days</li>
            </ul>
            <a href="/buy" class="btn btn-primary">Get PixlPut</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="faq" class="faq container">
        <h2>Questions</h2>
        <details>
            <summary>What does PixlPut need to run?</summary>
            <p>macOS 14 Sonoma or later, and the Accessibility permission, which PixlPut asks for on first launch. macOS requires that permission for any app that moves other apps' windows.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
            <summary>Can it move windows back to a different Desktop?</summary>
            <p>Yes, with <a href="https://github.com/asmvik/yabai">yabai</a> installed. macOS doesn't let one app move another app's window to a different Desktop, and yabai is the open-source tool that can. Its scripting addition needs System Integrity Protection partially disabled. Without yabai, PixlPut still restores every window's position and size on each Desktop. Windows that macOS moved to the wrong Desktop stay there, and restore after a restart relies on yabai.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
            <summary>Will it ask to control my browser?</summary>
            <p>Only if you turn on "Identify individual browser/editor windows" in Settings. It's off by default, and while it's off PixlPut never asks for Automation permission for any other app.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
            <summary>Does it take screenshots?</summary>
            <p>Only if you opt in. Space thumbnails help you recognize saved layouts. They're off by default, need explicit consent, and are stored on your Mac only.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
            <summary>What happens when the trial ends?</summary>
            <p>Capture and restore pause until you enter a license key. Your saved layouts stay on your Mac, ready for when you do.</p>
        </details>
    </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
    .hero {
        padding: 96px 0 64px;
        text-align: center;
    }
    .hero h1 {
        font-size: clamp(40px, 6vw, 64px);
        line-height: 1.05;
        letter-spacing: -0.025em;
        font-weight: 700;
        margin-bottom: 24px;
    }
    .hero .lede {
        font-size: 19px;
        line-height: 1.55;
        color: var(--text-dim);
        max-width: 720px;
        margin: 0 auto 32px;
    }
    .cta-row {
        display: flex;
        gap: 12px;
        justify-content: center;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        margin-bottom: 16px;
    }
    .micro {
        font-size: 13px;
        color: var(--text-dim);
    }

    .features {
        padding: 80px 0;
    }
    .features h2, .how h2, .pricing h2, .faq h2 {
        font-size: 32px;
        font-weight: 700;
        letter-spacing: -0.02em;
        margin-bottom: 32px;
    }
    .features .grid {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
        gap: 20px;
    }
    .features article {
        background: var(--surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: 14px;
        padding: 24px;
    }
    .features h3 {
        font-size: 17px;
        margin-bottom: 8px;
        font-weight: 600;
    }
    .features p {
        color: var(--text-dim);
        font-size: 15px;
        line-height: 1.5;
    }

    .how {
        padding: 64px 0;
    }
    .how ol {
        padding-left: 24px;
        max-width: 720px;
    }
    .how li {
        margin-bottom: 16px;
        color: var(--text-dim);
        font-size: 16px;
        line-height: 1.55;
    }
    .how li strong { color: var(--text); }
    .how .aside {
        margin-top: 32px;
        padding: 16px 20px;
        background: var(--surface);
        border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
        border-radius: 6px;
        color: var(--text-dim);
        font-size: 14px;
    }

    .pricing {
        padding: 80px 0;
    }
    .plan {
        max-width: 480px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        background: var(--surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: 16px;
        padding: 32px;
        text-align: center;
    }
    .plan .price {
        font-size: 56px;
        font-weight: 700;
        letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    }
    .plan .meta {
        color: var(--text-dim);
        margin-bottom: 24px;
    }
    .plan ul {
        list-style: none;
        text-align: left;
        margin: 0 0 28px;
        padding: 0;
    }
    .plan li {
        padding: 8px 0;
        color: var(--text-dim);
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
        font-size: 14px;
    }
    .plan li:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

    .faq {
        padding: 64px 0;
        max-width: 760px;
    }
    .faq details {
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
        padding: 18px 0;
    }
    .faq summary {
        cursor: pointer;
        font-weight: 600;
        font-size: 16px;
    }
    .faq details p {
        margin-top: 12px;
        color: var(--text-dim);
        font-size: 15px;
        line-height: 1.6;
    }
</style>
